{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS launches Sustainability console for carbon emissions tracking - AWS","context":"AWS has introduced the AWS Sustainability console, a new, standalone service that enables sustainability professionals to access carbon emissions data from their AWS usage without requiring billing permissions. This service builds upon the features of the Customer Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT) in the AWS Billing console. The console provides customers with their estimated carbon emissions from using AWS, calculated using market-based and location-based methods, and available by AWS Region, service, and emissions scope (1, 2, 3). Additional capabilities include customizable visualizations, the ability to set the fiscal year start month, customizable CSV reports, and API/SDK access for seamless integration of emissions data into existing reporting workflows. The AWS Sustainability console is currently available in the US East (N. Virginia) region and provides carbon emissions data for all AWS commercial regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/03/aws-launches-sustainability-console/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS’ Customer Carbon Footprint Tool now includes Scope 3 emissions data - AWS","context":"AWS has updated its Customer Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT) to include Scope 3 emissions data and Scope 1 natural gas and refrigerants. This expansion allows AWS customers to have a more comprehensive view of their cloud carbon footprint, covering all three industry-standard emission scopes as defined by the Greenhouse Gas Protocol. The update provides visibility into the lifecycle carbon impact of AWS usage, including emissions from manufacturing servers, powering AWS facilities, and transporting equipment to data centers. Historical data is available from January 2022, enabling organizations to track their progress and make informed decisions about their cloud strategy to meet sustainability goals. The updated CCFT data is accessible through the dashboard and AWS Billing and Cost Management Data Exports, allowing for easy integration into operational workflows, sustainability planning, and reporting processes. For more information, visit the AWS Billing and Cost Management console or read the updated methodology documentation and release notes","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/10/aws-customer-carbon-footprint-tool-scope-3-emissions-data/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS Sustainability service now includes water withdrawals data - AWS","context":"AWS Sustainability, a service offered by Amazon Web Services (AWS), has expanded its capabilities to include annual water withdrawals data for workloads. This enhancement enables organizations to obtain a comprehensive understanding of their environmental impact, as it complements the existing carbon emissions data. The water withdrawals data is accessible through the AWS Sustainability console and API, and is available on an annual basis by AWS Region, service, and AWS account. The data represents the total volume of water withdrawn for data center operations, with efficiency improvements reflected as lower withdrawal volumes. The water withdrawals data is provided at no additional cost in all AWS Regions where the service is available. To get started, visit the AWS Sustainability user guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/aws-sustainability-water-withdrawals/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles","skill_description":"Configures EC2 instances to securely call AWS services by creating and attaching IAM roles via instance profiles, eliminating hardcoded credentials. Use when an EC2 instance needs permissions to access AWS services like S3, DynamoDB, SQS, or CloudWatch through temporary credentials.\n\nServices: ec2, iam\nTasks: configure, secure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"signing-in-to-aws","skill_description":"Gets AWS credentials for CLI/SDK access via `aws login`. Activates when a developer needs to authenticate to AWS for local development, when an AWS operation fails due to missing or expired credentials, or when someone asks about setting up AWS access. Triggers: \"set up AWS\", \"configure AWS\", \"aws login\", \"get credentials\", \"authenticate\", \"session expired\", \"token expired\", \"no credentials\", \"AccessDeniedException\" with no configured credentials.\n\n\nServices: signin, iam, sts\nTasks: authenticate, configure, credential-management\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: local-development\n*","skill_name":"signing-in-to-aws"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify whether these AWS sustainability API operations and IAM permissions exist."}
